Q3. Is the basic Singleton implementation
thread-safe?

Interview Ready Answer

No.

The basic Singleton implementation
(Lazy Initialization)

is NOT Thread-Safe.

Reason

If multiple threads call
getInstance() simultaneously,

both may find

instance == null

and both can create
separate objects.

This violates the Singleton Pattern.

------------------------------------------------------------

Thread-Safe Solution

Use synchronized.

public static synchronized Singleton getInstance(){

    if(instance==null){

        instance=new Singleton();
    }

    return instance;
}

The synchronized keyword ensures
that only one thread can execute
the method at a time.

------------------------------------------------------------

Note

Although synchronized makes
Singleton thread-safe,

it may reduce performance because
every call acquires a lock.

For better performance,

Java provides

• Double Checked Locking
• Enum Singleton

